# Project rules

## Where logic lives

- Business logic belongs in dbt models. DAX measures only aggregate or format dbt outputs.
  Never recompute IRR, TVPI or PME in DAX. The Streamlit app holds no business logic either;
  it reads marts.
- Every metric formula must match the docs and must be covered by a Python reconciliation test.

## Data honesty

- Never fabricate data, metrics, benchmarks or screenshots. An unknown or uncomputable value is
  NULL, not 0. An IRR that fails to converge returns None and surfaces as NULL — never a fake 0,
  which would read as "broke even" rather than "could not be computed".
- Report only numbers measured from a real run. If something was designed but not deployed, label
  it "designed, not deployed".

## Modelling standards

- Every new mart needs four things: a declared grain, an enforced contract, uniqueness and
  not-null tests on that grain, and YAML docs stating the formula in plain English, including the
  net-vs-gross and as-of conventions.
- Keep the DuckDB and Snowflake builds both passing. Use `adapter.dispatch` for dialect
  differences rather than writing engine-specific SQL inline.

## Security

- All credentials come from environment variables. Never commit secrets, account identifiers or
  `.env` files. Mask account identifiers and key paths in any error text that reaches stdout or a
  CI log.

## Workflow

- Small commits with conventional commit messages. One feature per branch. Open a PR for each
  phase.
- When unsure about existing behaviour, read the code and `docs/AUDIT.md` before changing
  anything.

## Attribution

- This repository started from a fork of another project. Anything adapted from it must keep its
  "Adapted from" header and must be listed in `ATTRIBUTION.md` with the change described.
